[Officium]
Saints Hippolytus and Cassian, Martyrs

[Oratio]
Grant, we beseech Thee, almighty God: that the venerable solemnity of Thy blessed Martyrs Hippolytus and Cassian may avail us to the increase both of devotion toward Thee, and healthfulness to our own souls.
$Per Dominum

[Lectio93]
Hippolytus, baptized by Saint Laurence, while receiving the Eucharist in his own house, was seized and brought before the Emperor Valerian; being questioned by him concerning the profession of his religion, he freely confessed himself a Christian. Wherefore he was beaten with clubs, but when his faith was found only the bolder under the blows, he was tempted with promises of gifts and honours. Then when words were found only to be thrown away upon him, he was given over to the Prefect to be put to death. The Praefect went to the house of Hippolytus to take possession of his goods, and there found that all the household were Christians. He strove in vain to awe them into the denial of their faith, and then ordered Concordia, the nurse of Hippolytus, who was encouraging the rest, to be beaten to death with whips loaded with lead, then he ordereth the rest to be slain outside the Tiburtine gate [towards Tivoli]. Hippolytus was tied to wild horses which dragged him through rough places full of briars and thistles, until with a mangled body he resigned his soul to God. Justin the Priest buried him along with the others in the Veranian field. On the same day, at the Forum of Sulla, Cassian the Martyr was afflicted with a most cruel punishment; who, his hands being bound behind his back, is delivered to be pierced and torn to pieces with the iron styles of the boys whom he was instructing. Owing to the weakness of the means, the suffering of his martyrdom was very grievous and long, and his palm all the more glorious.
